  {
   "id": "JZ1aQ5FuBwAJ",
   "from": "Chris Paciorek",
   "author": "5fa2a363d4ddd1d1",
   "date": "2018-11-17T21:46:03Z",
   "subject": "Re: hierarchical Poisson regression---top-level posteriors very wide",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "Hi Chris, this doesn&#39;t look like anything related to NIMBLE but rather\r<br>with your model structure.\r<br>\r<br>Considering just your K=1 case (the K&gt;1 should be similar)...\r<br>\r<br>When you have:\r<br>B[m] ~ dnorm(mean=B_hat,sd=1000)\r<br>\r<br>you&#39;re basically saying that the B[m] values can be very far from\r<br>B_hat. Given this wide variance, you can&#39;t learn much about B_hat.\r<br>Even if your data could pin down each B[m] without any uncertainty,\r<br>you only have 7 B[m] values to try to learn about B_hat in a situation\r<br>where you say with certainty that the signal to noise ratio is very\r<br>low (sd=1000).\r<br>\r<br>Given the normality here, we know analytically that the posterior\r<br>variance for B_hat is 1 / (1/1000^2 + 7/1000^2) = 125000 (e.g., see\r<br>Gelman et al Bayesian data analysis book, section 2.5).\r<br>\r<br>The standard model structure here would be to have something like:\r<br>B[m] ~ dnorm(mean=B_hat,sd=tau)\r<br>and put a prior on tau  (e.g., tau ~ dunif(0, some_big_value)) or a\r<br>half-Cauchy distribution). That way you can learn how close the B[m]\r<br>values are to the B_hat based on the data.  And then, presuming that\r<br>tau is not estimated to be too large, you&#39;ll be able to learn about\r<br>B_hat from what you can learn about the B[m] values.\r<br>\r<br>Side note: you don&#39;t really need inverse(). You can just do:\r<br>A_hat &lt;- diag(x=rep(1/1000,K))\r<br>And in fact since A_hat is constant, you could just initialize it\r<br>outside the model and pass it in as either &#39;inits&#39; or &#39;constants&#39;.\r<br>Doesn&#39;t matter much with small K and because A_hat is never updated in\r<br>the MCMC, but just a note.\r<br>\r<br>Second side note: I would not initialize A inside the loop, since it\r<br>is not indexed by &#39;m&#39;. The result is that you are asking nimble to\r<br>create the same node multiple times. Doesn&#39;t cause a problem here, but\r<br>it could lead to problems in other situations.\r<br>\r<br>Chris\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "On Fri, Nov 16, 2018 at 5:10 PM &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">w.cca...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; Hi All,\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; I&#39;m trying to build a simple hierarchical Poisson regression model. I can make it work (i.e., return reasonably narrow regression coefficient posteriors close to true values) for individual models/data, but the top-level posteriors for the regression coefficients are very wide and the means aren&#39;t at the true values. It&#39;s odd to me b/c in the simulated data (see code below) the number of observations for each model is lower than the number of individual models and, yet, the posteriors for the individual models are good while the posterior describing the top-level distribution (of regression coefficients) is bad. Any ideas?\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; Code:\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; poisHRegCode &lt;- nimbleCode({\r<br>&gt;    if(K &gt; 1){\r<br>&gt;       A_hat[1:K,1:K] &lt;- inverse(diag(x=rep(1000,K)))\r<br>&gt;       multimu[1:K] &lt;- rep(0,K)\r<br>&gt;       B_hat[1:K] ~ dmnorm(mean=multimu[1:K], prec=A_hat[1:K,1:K])\r<br>&gt;    }\r<br>&gt;    else{\r<br>&gt;       B_hat ~ dnorm(mean=0,sd=1000)\r<br>&gt;    }\r<br>&gt;    for(m in 1:M){\r<br>&gt;       if(K &gt; 1){\r<br>&gt;          A[1:K,1:K] &lt;- inverse(diag(x=rep(1000,K)))\r<br>&gt;          B[1:K,m] ~ dmnorm(mean=B_hat[1:K], prec=A[1:K,1:K])\r<br>&gt;       }else{\r<br>&gt;          B[m] ~ dnorm(mean=B_hat,sd=1000)\r<br>&gt;       }\r<br>&gt;    }\r<br>&gt;    for (m in 1:M){\r<br>&gt;       for (n in 1:N){\r<br>&gt;          if(K &gt; 1){\r<br>&gt;             lambda[n,m] &lt;- inprod(X[n,1:K,m], B[1:K,m])\r<br>&gt;          }else{\r<br>&gt;             lambda[n,m] &lt;- X[n,m] * B[m]\r<br>&gt;          }\r<br>&gt;          Y[n,m] ~ dpois(exp(lambda[n,m]))\r<br>&gt;       }\r<br>&gt;    }\r<br>&gt; })\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; N &lt;- 6\r<br>&gt; M &lt;- 7\r<br>&gt; X_list &lt;- lapply(1:M,function(x)c(rep(1,10),rnorm(10)))\r<br>&gt; X &lt;- array(unlist(X_list),dim=c(N,2,M))\r<br>&gt; Bs &lt;- rnorm(n=M,mean=0.5,sd=0.1)\r<br>&gt; L &lt;- c()\r<br>&gt; for(j in 1:length(Bs)){\r<br>&gt;    L &lt;- cbind(L,apply(X[,,j],1,function(k,b)return(k[1] + b * k[2]),b=Bs[j]))\r<br>&gt; }\r<br>&gt; Y &lt;- apply(L,2,function(l)sapply(l,function(j)rpois(lambda=exp(j),n=1)))\r<br>&gt; K &lt;- 2\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; poisHRegData &lt;- list(Y=Y,\r<br>&gt;                   X=X)\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; poisHRegConsts &lt;- list(N=N,\r<br>&gt;                      K=K,\r<br>&gt;                      M=M)\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; poisHRegInits &lt;- list(B_hat=rep(0,K))\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; poisHRegModel &lt;- nimbleModel(code=poisHRegCode,\r<br>&gt;                         data=poisHRegData,\r<br>&gt;                         inits=poisHRegInits,\r<br>&gt;                         constants=poisHRegConsts)\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; #compile nimble model to C++ code—much faster runtime\r<br>&gt; C_poisHRegModel &lt;- compileNimble(poisHRegModel, showCompilerOutput = FALSE)\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; #configure the MCMC\r<br>&gt; poisHRegModel_conf &lt;- configureMCMC(poisHRegModel)\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; #add monitors\r<br>&gt; poisHRegModel_conf$addMonitors(c(&quot;B&quot;))\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; #build MCMC\r<br>&gt; poisHRegModelMCMC &lt;- buildMCMC(poisHRegModel_conf,thin=1,enableWAIC = TRUE)\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; #compile MCMC to C++—much faster\r<br>&gt; C_poisHRegModelMCMC &lt;- compileNimble(poisHRegModelMCMC,project=poisHRegModel)\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; #number of MCMC iterations\r<br>&gt; niter=10000\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; #set seed for replicability\r<br>&gt; set.seed(1)\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; #call the C++ compiled MCMC model\r<br>&gt; C_poisHRegModelMCMC$run(niter)\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; #save the MCMC chain (monitored variables) as a matrix\r<br>&gt; samples &lt;- as.matrix(C_poisHRegModelMCMC$mvSamples)\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; WAIC &lt;- C_poisHRegModelMCMC$calculateWAIC(nburnin=1000)\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "&gt; --\r<br>&gt; You received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups &quot;nimble-users&quot; group.\r<br>&gt; To unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">nimble-users...@googlegroups.com</a>.\r<br>&gt; To post to this group, send email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">nimble...@googlegroups.com</a>.\r<br>&gt; To view this discussion on the web visit <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/msgid/nimble-users/008ad40f-6809-4341-83d9-3472acbcdc01%40googlegroups.com\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/d/msgid/nimble-users/008ad40f-6809-4341-83d9-3472acbcdc01%2540googlegroups.com&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792478715603000&amp;usg=AOvVaw175fDJVm_VtqWAT5grScEi\">https://groups.google.com/d/msgid/nimble-users/008ad40f-6809-4341-83d9-3472acbcdc01%40googlegroups.com</a>.\r<br>&gt; For more options, visit <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/optout\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/d/optout&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792478715603000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3PY0Mc9yGIVfzVObDj6eHZ\">https://groups.google.com/d/optout</a>.\r<br>"
